# Template for docker/.env-local — copy this file and fill it in:
#
#   cp docker/.env-local.example docker/.env-local && chmod 600 docker/.env-local
#
# docker/.env-local is gitignored and optional. It is loaded after docker/.env by
# docker-compose.yml (dev) and docker-compose-non-dev.yml (nondev), and later files
# win — so anything set here overrides the committed defaults.
#
# It is NOT loaded by docker-compose-secure.yml, deliberately: local overrides must
# not be able to weaken a secure deployment. Use docker/.env-secure for that
# (docker/.env-secure.example is its template).
#
# Real secrets and account details go here, never in docker/.env — that file is
# tracked by git.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Superset secret key
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker/.env ships SUPERSET_SECRET_KEY=TEST_NON_DEV_SECRET, which is committed and
# therefore public. Override it here so this instance has its own.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 42
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING AN EXISTING INSTANCE. The key encrypts the stored
# passwords of every database connection Superset knows about. Changing it on a
# stack that already has connections makes them undecryptable — they fail with a
# decryption error, not a clear message.
#
# On a fresh instance (no database connections saved yet): just set it.
#
# On an existing instance, re-encrypt rather than just replace.
#
# Keep a note of the old key, set the new one below, then:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose-non-dev.yml up -d db redis
#   docker compose -f docker-compose-non-dev.yml run --rm --no-deps superset superset re-encrypt-secrets --previous_secret_key '<the old key>'
#   docker compose -f docker-compose-non-dev.yml up -d
#
# This also works as a recovery if you already restarted and init is failing —
# re-encrypt-secrets reads the columns with raw SQL and never hits the ORM path
# that throws. Take a backup first:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose-non-dev.yml exec -T db \
#     pg_dump -U superset -d superset > superset-before-rotate.sql
#
# Back the value up somewhere outside this repo. Lose it and every saved database
# connection has to be re-entered by hand.

# SUPERSET_SECRET_KEY=<openssl rand -base64 42>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email (SMTP) for alerts and reports — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Uncomment and fill in the whole block to enable email. Leave it commented out
# and Superset simply never sends mail.
#
# SMTP_USER/SMTP_PASSWORD are only needed if your relay requires authentication;
# Superset skips the SMTP login when either is empty.
#
# SMTP_MAIL_FROM must be a full address — it is split on "@" when the report is
# sent, so a bare username fails there rather than at startup.

# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_STARTTLS=true
# SMTP_SSL_SERVER_AUTH=true
# SMTP_SSL=false
# SMTP_USER=<smtp-username>
# SMTP_PASSWORD=<smtp-password>
# SMTP_MAIL_FROM=noreply@example.com
# EMAIL_REPORTS_SUBJECT_PREFIX=[Superset]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment mode — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker/.env ships SUPERSET_ENV=development. Set it here instead of editing that
# file, so the change stays out of git.
#
# It is not just a label. superset/config.py sets
#
#   RATELIMIT_ENABLED = os.environ.get("SUPERSET_ENV") == "production"
#
# so "development" leaves Superset's rate limiting off. Use "production" if the
# instance is reachable by anyone but you — nondev on a shared network, say.
# Mode 3 does not read this file: docker/.env-secure pins SUPERSET_ENV=production
# and superset_config_secure.py sets RATELIMIT_ENABLED = True outright.
#
# It also drives the environment tag in the UI header. ENVIRONMENT_TAG_CONFIG
# (superset/config.py) keys off this variable: "development" renders a red
# "Development" badge in the navbar, "production" sets empty text and hides it.
# Rendered by superset/views/base.py. Note the fallback — with no recognised value
# and FLASK_DEBUG on, the badge reads "flask-debug" instead.
# FLASK_DEBUG in docker/.env is a separate switch. It stays true in modes 1 and 2,
# and is what makes Superset swap in the permissive TALISMAN_DEV_CONFIG.

# SUPERSET_ENV=development

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin account — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker/.env does not set this at all; docker-init.sh falls back to "admin"
# (ADMIN_PASSWORD="${ADMIN_PASSWORD:-admin}"). Set it here rather than in
# docker/.env, which is tracked by git.
#
# ONLY APPLIES ON FIRST INIT. docker-init.sh runs `superset fab create-admin`, and
# flask_appbuilder/cli.py bails with "Error! User already exists admin" if the
# account is there — it does not reset the password. Changing this value on a stack
# that already initialised does nothing.
#
# To change it on a running stack, use the UI, or:
#
#   docker compose -f <compose-file> exec superset \
#       superset fab reset-password --username admin --password '<new>'
#
# Mode 3 does not read this file. docker/.env-secure carries its own generated
# ADMIN_PASSWORD, and docker-init.sh refuses to start secure mode if it is "admin".

# ADMIN_PASSWORD=<choose-one>
